            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Hi! Not a \"silly noob lua mistake\" at all. I fell into the same mistake when was writing tests for this module, but forgot to clarify this in documentation.\r\n\r\n`MiniPairs.map_buf()` and `MiniPairs.unmap_buf()` are mere wrappers for `vim.api.nvim_buf_set_keymap()` and `vim.api.nvim_buf_del_keymap()` respectively which also unregister pair for `<CR>` and `<BS>` (so that they don't act on that pair). In turn, Neovim's `nvim_buf_del_keymap()` *deletes buffer-local mapping* and not *makes global mapping not work for buffer*. So in this sense, `MiniPairs.unmap_buf()` will delete mapping which was done with `MiniPairs.map_buf()` and not `MiniPairs.map()` (used in `MiniPairs.setup()`).\r\n\r\nSo in your case there are two solutions:\r\n- *Easy one* is to make `'` work as by default but leave `<BS>` (and possibly `<CR>`) respect `''` pair: `inoremap <buffer> ' '`. So to do this for only Lisp files, add this Lua code: `vim.cmd([[au FileType lisp inoremap <buffer> ' ']])`.\r\n- *Complicated one* is to also make `<BS>` and `<CR>` don't act on `''` pair:\r\n    ```lua\r\n  require('mini.pairs').setup({ modes = { insert = true, command = true, terminal = true } })\r\n  MiniPairs.unmap('i', \"'\", \"''\")\r\n  vim.cmd(\r\n    [[au BufEnter * lua MiniPairs.map_buf(0, 'i', \"'\", { action = 'closeopen', pair = \"''\", neigh_pattern = '[^%a\\\\].', register = { cr = false } })]]\r\n  )\r\n  vim.cmd([[au FileType lisp lua MiniPairs.unmap_buf(0, 'i', \"'\", \"''\")]])\r\n    ```\r\n    This reverts global mapping from `setup()`, makes autocommands to make *buffer-local* mapping for every buffer and remove it when 'lisp' filetype is set.",
